#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# array_depth

#% text
# `array_depth(x)` gives the number of levels of `x` that form a full array.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the length of `dimensions(x)`: 1 for a vector, 2 for a matrix, 0 for an atom.

#% item
# `depth` counts the deepest nesting instead, ragged or not.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
array_depth([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
array_depth([1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A ragged list is a full array only at its first level, while `depth` sees all of it:

#% cell
x = [[1, 2], [3]]
[array_depth(x), depth(x)]
#% output
#= [1, 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `dimensions`, `depth`, `is_matrix`
